      "ported": [
        {
          "symbol": "ArgumentLazinessProfile::FallbackOnError",
          "to": "crates/calc-func/src/catalogue.rs ArgumentDemand::FallbackOnError (with ArgumentDemand::is_lazy, ArgumentDemand::discriminator, ArgumentDemand::holds_back); crates/calc-func/src/catalogue/error_fallback.rs FAMILY (IFERROR and IFNA declare it)",
          "changes": [
            {
              "change": "The variant joins calc-func's ArgumentDemand (cfunc-m1.1's port of the same axis, named for its caller) and answers holds_back(index) as index > 0, beside the discriminator Some(0) the pinned enum gives.",
              "reason": "Every lazy entry must answer what the declaration's readers ask of it, and these are the readers. The catalogue's declaration check (crates/calc-func/src/catalogue.rs, check_declaration, which the compiler runs over every entry when it evaluates the static tables BY_ID and BY_SURFACE) requires a lazy entry's discriminator to be a required argument, and a lazy kernel exactly when is_lazy holds. The case cfunc_m1_1__catalogue_consistency asks every lazy entry for its discriminator first and for at most one argument that holds_back marks (the lazy entries are IF, IFERROR and IFNA). In calc-fml, holds_back is read only by carries_final_sum (crates/calc-fml/src/eval/compile.rs), for IF's BranchOnCondition alone (it answers false for FallbackOnError), and discriminator is not read at all. calc-fml schedules IFERROR's and IFNA's arguments by the kernel's requests, not by these answers: compile gives each argument of an entry whose demand is_lazy a block of its own, and a block runs only when the kernel asks LazyArguments for that argument (crates/calc-fml/src/eval/run.rs, Blocks::argument). dc_ibp2__error_fallback witnesses that a fallback is evaluated only on an error the entry catches."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
